1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,380
You've read an English sentence up to one hidden character.

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:08,220
With yes-or-no questions, how many do you need per character, on average?

3
00:00:10,066 --> 00:00:11,816
The natural answer is five.

4
00:00:12,196 --> 00:00:17,816
Each answer can cut what's left in half, and five cuts take twenty-seven down to one.

5
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:23,360
The ideal is log base two of twenty-seven, about four point seven five.

6
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:28,720
In 1951, Claude Shannon estimated the true figure at about one.

7
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:37,000
Whatever it is, no text compressor can beat it on average, and guessing what comes next is how language models are trained.

8
00:00:37,433 --> 00:00:40,133
So where do four of every five questions go?

9
00:00:40,513 --> 00:00:44,673
Splitting the alphabet evenly charges every character the same price.

10
00:00:45,053 --> 00:00:50,463
Z costs as much as E, yet E turns up a hundred and thirty times as often.

11
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:55,490
Instead, ask questions that split the probability, not the alphabet.

12
00:00:55,870 --> 00:00:58,400
Is it a space, E, T, A, or O?

13
00:00:58,780 --> 00:01:01,460
Those five make up nearly half of all text.

14
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:03,790
E starts near a tenth of the bar.

15
00:01:04,170 --> 00:01:09,760
Each ideal answer keeps half the probability, so E doubles its share of what's left.

16
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:13,930
Three point three ideal doublings, and E fills the whole bar.

17
00:01:14,266 --> 00:01:18,876
Z starts at one in twelve hundred and sixty, and needs ten point three.

18
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:26,450
In general, a character with probability p needs k questions, where p times two to the k equals one.

19
00:01:27,950 --> 00:01:34,080
So two to the k equals one over p, and k equals log base two of one over p.

20
00:01:34,433 --> 00:01:37,303
Now give each character its own cost as a height.

21
00:01:37,666 --> 00:01:43,656
The widths add up to one, so the total area is the average number of questions per character.

22
00:01:44,036 --> 00:01:47,416
Pour the skyline flat, and its height is that average.

23
00:01:47,766 --> 00:01:53,686
Shannon called that average the entropy, counted in bits, one bit per yes-or-no answer.

24
00:01:54,066 --> 00:01:55,676
For English letters, it's

25
00:01:55,976 --> 00:01:57,336
four point one one.

26
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:06,790
That beats four point seven five, but it's still four times Shannon's estimate: it prices every character as if nothing came before it.

27
00:02:07,133 --> 00:02:08,633
What comes after a Q?

28
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:13,330
It's U, ninety-eight times out of a hundred.

29
00:02:13,710 --> 00:02:20,810
U swallows the bar, its cost drops to almost nothing, and the average falls to point one six bits.

30
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:27,460
After TH, E takes two thirds of the bar, and its cost shrinks to about half a question.

31
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:37,740
Half a question, like three point three doublings, sounds impossible, but a compressor asks about long runs of letters, and the fractions add up.

32
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:41,250
After TH, the average is one point seven.

33
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:46,220
But averaged over every two-letter context, it's still two point eight.

34
00:02:46,566 --> 00:02:55,026
Longer contexts need more text than any corpus holds, so Shannon used a predictor that already knows English: a person.

35
00:02:55,366 --> 00:03:00,336
His subject read a passage letter by letter, guessing each next one until right.

36
00:03:00,716 --> 00:03:04,236
Seventy-nine of a hundred and two came on the first guess.

37
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:09,840
A twin who guesses the same way could rebuild the passage from these counts alone.

38
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:16,570
Treat each guess count as a symbol, and the same skyline pours flat at about one point four bits.

39
00:03:16,950 --> 00:03:19,050
A better guesser could only lower it.

40
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:27,060
Shannon's tests, with a hundred letters of context, put English between point six and one point three bits per character.

41
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:40,740
In twenty twenty-three, a language model compressing part of a book that reached Project Gutenberg after its training, in the same twenty-seven symbols, needed point eight four bits, inside that range.

42
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:45,590
The hidden character from the start is the Y at the end of the other day.

43
00:03:45,970 --> 00:03:50,560
Shannon's subject got it on the first guess, a single yes-or-no question.

44
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:52,870
Splitting the alphabet took five.

45
00:03:53,250 --> 00:03:56,720
In English, almost no letter arrives without a past.

